---
// The Crisis: Solar Depletion. Built verbatim from the copy deck at
// docs/copy/crisis-solar-depletion.md (deck v1). Crisis surface.
import Shell from '../../layouts/Shell.astro';

const STATS = [
  { value: '600M tons', label: 'Hydrogen the Sun consumes every second', note: 'This rate of loss continues around the clock with no independent oversight.' },
  { value: '8m 20s', label: 'Time sunlight takes to reach Earth', note: 'A delivery record maintained for billions of years without one written acknowledgment from the recipient.' },
  { value: '4.6B years', label: 'Time already elapsed', note: 'We have already wasted approximately 4.6 billion years.' },
  { value: '4.8B years', label: 'Time remaining, organizational planning estimate', note: 'Ratified by the Board of Trustees. Scientific ranges appear in the section above.' },
  { value: '365', label: 'Confirmed solar appearances last year', note: 'Logged daily by the Office of Solar Continuity against an annual target of 365.' },
  { value: '0', label: 'Solar extinction events since the Foundation’s founding', note: 'A record the Foundation intends to protect and, where appropriate, cite.' },
];
---

<Shell
  title="Solar Depletion · The Crisis · Solar Save Foundation"
  description="Every second, the Sun consumes roughly 600 million tons of hydrogen it can never recover. The Solar Save Foundation exists because someone had to put the matter in writing."
  surface="crisis"
>
  <article>
    <header class="crisis-hero">
      <div class="inner">
        <p class="eyebrow">The Crisis · Briefing one of six</p>
        <h1>The Sun Is Spending Its Principal</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Every second, our star consumes roughly 600 million tons of
          hydrogen that it can never recover. It has kept this pace without
          oversight, without recognition, and without a succession plan.
          The Solar Save Foundation exists because someone had to put the
          matter in writing.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="science">
      <div class="inner narrow">
        <figure class="science-figure">
          <img
            src="/images/sun-deep-space.jpg"
            alt="The Sun suspended against deep space"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption class="mono">
            THE SUBJECT, PHOTOGRAPHED WITHOUT ITS KNOWLEDGE OR CONSENT.
            IMAGERY REVIEWED BY THE SOLAR WELLNESS MONITORING TEAM.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <h2>The Science</h2>
        <p>
          The Sun formed approximately 4.6 billion years ago from the
          gravitational collapse of a cloud of gas and dust, and it has
          remained in continuous operation ever since. In all that time it
          has never requested a maintenance window, a work ethic the
          Foundation regards as admirable and, on the evidence,
          unsustainable. Today it sits roughly midway through its main
          sequence lifetime, the long stable period in which a star fuses
          hydrogen into helium at its core. Midway is a word that reassures
          many people. The Foundation's Scientific Council has reviewed the
          word and was not reassured.
        </p>
        <p>
          The physics is not in dispute. At the core, where temperatures
          reach roughly 15 million degrees Celsius, the Sun converts about
          600 million tons of hydrogen into helium every second, releasing
          the energy that arrives here as daylight, harvests, and the
          modern economy. Roughly four million tons of solar mass simply
          cease to exist each second, converted to energy under a formula
          the Sun did not negotiate and has never contested. This is a
          drawdown of finite reserves. No mainstream astronomer describes
          it as a renewable arrangement.
        </p>
        <p>
          The scientific consensus is that about five billion years of main
          sequence hydrogen remain before the Sun enters its red giant
          phase, expanding past the orbits of Mercury and Venus with
          consequences for this planet that the literature describes calmly
          and the Foundation does not. Solar luminosity is already
          increasing by roughly 1 percent every 100 million years, which
          means the Sun is working harder each era even as its reserves
          decline, a pattern familiar to anyone who has watched a family
          business in its later years. These figures are real, peer
          reviewed, and freely available. They have been available for
          decades. Availability has not been the problem.
        </p>
        <p>
          For planning purposes the Foundation uses a figure of 4.8 billion
          years remaining. This is an organizational planning estimate,
          adopted by the Board of Trustees after a two year review, and it
          is deliberately more conservative than the scientific range
          because stewardship demands a margin. It is not a scientific
          measurement and is not presented as one. It is presented as a
          deadline, because institutions do not move for ranges.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="numbers">
      <div class="inner">
        <h2>Depletion by the Numbers</h2>
        <dl class="stat-grid">
          {STATS.map((s) => (
            <div class="stat">
              <dd class="mono stat-value">{s.value}</dd>
              <dt class="stat-label">{s.label}</dt>
              <p class="stat-note">{s.note}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </dl>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="doing">
      <div class="inner narrow">
        <h2>What the Foundation Is Doing</h2>
        <p>
          The Foundation's first responsibility is vigilance. Our Solar
          Wellness Monitoring program confirms the Sun's appearance every
          morning, logs the finding, and releases it to the public at no
          charge, because transparency is a family value. Last year this
          process succeeded 365 times out of a possible 365, a performance
          the Foundation credits to disciplined procedure and declines to
          credit to chance.
        </p>
        <p>
          Vigilance alone is not a plan, which is why the Foundation
          maintains one. The Solar Continuity Strategic Framework has now
          entered its third phase, with 27 preliminary depletion
          assessments completed this fiscal year and stakeholder alignment
          sessions scheduled through the coming quarter. Each assessment
          brings the crisis into sharper administrative focus. The hydrogen
          situation has not yet responded to the framework, and the
          Foundation has budgeted for that patience.
        </p>
        <p>
          Beyond the science, the Foundation defends the Sun where it
          cannot defend itself, in the arena of public opinion.
          Organizations that describe 4.8 billion years as plenty of time
          are well funded, well rested, and active after dark. Our response
          is measured, lawful, and documented in triplicate. The nighttime
          lobby has never disclosed its donor list, and the Moon, which
          produces no light of its own, has offered no comment. The
          Foundation also continues direct outreach to the Sun itself. The
          relationship remains unacknowledged, and we have never required
          gratitude to do our duty.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="waiting">
      <div class="inner narrow">
        <h2>The Cost of Waiting</h2>
        <p>
          The Sun loses four million tons of itself every second, and every
          second of public deliberation is a second the minutes of our
          meetings faithfully record. At the current rate, the Sun has
          never been closer to extinction. This is not alarmism. It is
          arithmetic, and arithmetic does not lobby.
        </p>
        <p>
          We inherited a working star. That is 4.6 billion years of
          tradition, of harvests, of daylight arriving on schedule, handed
          down to this generation without an invoice. The question before
          us is whether the inheritance reaches the next generation, or
          whether we become the people who received about five billion
          years of warning and used them to feel unhurried. Complacency is
          not a plan. It is simply the least expensive way to fail.
        </p>
        <blockquote>
          Scientists agree that doing nothing will not save the Sun.
        </blockquote>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="cta">
      <div class="inner">
        <h2>The Sun Cannot Save Itself</h2>
        <div class="cta-actions">
          <a class="btn-gold" href="/donate">Make a Commitment</a>
          <a class="btn-ghost" href="/mission/the-plan">Read the Strategic Framework</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Shell>

<style>
  .inner {
    max-width: var(--page-max);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: var(--space-6) var(--space-4);
  }

  .inner.narrow { max-width: 820px; }

  .crisis-hero {
    background:
      radial-gradient(900px 500px at 82% 0%, rgba(179, 38, 30, 0.18), transparent 60%),
      radial-gradient(700px 420px at 70% 30%, rgba(224, 163, 46, 0.14), transparent 55%),
      var(--navy-900);
    border-bottom: 1px solid #22385c;
  }

  h1 { font-size: var(--text-hero); max-width: 16ch; }

  .lede {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    color: #b9c4d6;
    max-width: 56ch;
  }

  h2 { font-size: var(--text-2xl); margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }

  .science-figure {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-5);
  }

  .science-figure img {
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    width: 100%;
  }

  .science-figure figcaption {
    font-size: 0.62rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    color: var(--text-faint);
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
  }

  .science p, .doing p, .waiting p { color: #b9c4d6; line-height: 1.75; }

  .numbers { background: var(--navy-950); border-top: 1px solid #16233c; border-bottom: 1px solid #16233c; }

  .stat-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-5) var(--space-4);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .stat { display: flex; flex-direction: column; }

  .stat-value {
    font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 4vw, 2.8rem);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--gold-300);
    line-height: 1;
    margin: 0;
    order: 1;
  }

  .stat-label {
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: #eef1f7;
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
    order: 2;
  }

  .stat-note {
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    color: #7a879c;
    margin: var(--space-1) 0 0;
    order: 3;
    max-width: 34ch;
  }

  blockquote {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--gold-500);
    margin: var(--space-5) 0 0;
    padding-left: var(--space-4);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--text-xl);
    font-style: italic;
    color: #eef1f7;
  }

  .cta { background: var(--navy-950); border-top: 1px solid #16233c; text-align: center; }
  .cta h2 { font-size: var(--text-3xl); margin-bottom: var(--space-5); }
  .cta-actions { display: flex; gap: var(--space-3); justify-content: center; flex-wrap: wrap; }

  .btn-gold,
  .btn-ghost {
    font: 700 0.92rem var(--font-sans);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    text-decoration: none;
    padding: 0.9rem 1.6rem;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
  }

  .btn-gold { background: var(--gold-500); color: var(--navy-950); }
  .btn-gold:hover { background: var(--gold-300); color: var(--navy-950); }
  .btn-ghost { border: 1px solid #3a5580; color: #eef1f7; }
  .btn-ghost:hover { border-color: var(--gold-300); color: var(--gold-300); }
</style>
